Let $P=(q,m,d)$ be admissible, put $n=md$, and let $e\geq0$.  Then
$E_b(P,e)>0$.  If $x\geq0$ and
\[
 x\leq10^{11}n^4E_{\mathrm{pair}}(P,e),
\]
then
\[
 \sqrt{x}\leq10^6n^2E_b(P,e),
 \qquad
 4\sqrt{x}<I_{\rm raw}(P,e).
\]
If in addition $I_{\rm raw}(P,e)<4$, then
\[
 \sqrt{x}<\frac4{1000},\qquad x<1.
\]
For $0\leq e\leq1$ one also has
\[
 \frac nq\leq n^2E_b(P,e),
 \qquad
 e\leq n^2E_b(P,e).
\]
On this probability domain, $I_{\rm raw}(P,e)<4$ further implies
\[
 \frac nq<\frac4{10^9}.
\]
Under the displayed extraction-scale bound, the same small branch also gives
\[
 472x+24\frac nq+192\sqrt{x}+344e<I_{\rm raw}(P,e).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
\leanok
\uses{thm:pauli-final-envelope-support}
The positive field term makes $E_b(P,e)$ strictly positive.  Taking the
square root of the extraction-scale bound produces the factor $n^2$ and halves
the envelope exponent.  The field ratio and the clipped error are absorbed by
the same positive base.  On the nonsaturated branch, the inequality
$I_{\rm raw}(P,e)<4$ gives the three strict numerical bounds, while positivity
of the base makes the state-component comparison strict.
\end{proof}

